---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Privacy Notice · The Space Between"
  description="Privacy notice for between.sonicboom.org.uk: what data we collect, how we use it, and your rights. Run by Sonic Boom Music CIC."
>
  <main id="main">

    <!-- ── Page hero ────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <div class="page-hero">
      <div class="container page-hero__inner">
        <a href="/" class="page-hero__back">
          <span aria-hidden="true">←</span> Back to the site
        </a>
        <h1 class="page-hero__title">Privacy notice</h1>
        <p class="page-hero__sub">
          What we collect, why, and what you can do about it. Plain English.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ── Content ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
    <div class="page-content">
      <div class="container page-content__inner">

        <div class="prose">

          <h2>Who we are</h2>
          <p>
            This site is run by <strong>Sonic Boom Music CIC</strong>, a community interest
            company registered in England and Wales. Our address and registration details
            are available at <a href="https://www.sonicboom.org.uk/?utm_source=between&utm_medium=privacy&utm_campaign=sonicboom" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">sonicboom.org.uk <span class="sr-only">(opens in new tab)</span></a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            Sonic Boom Music CIC is company number 15628568 and is registered with the ICO
            (ZB724102). We are the data controller for personal data collected through this website.
            This notice covers The Space Between website; our organisation-wide
            <a href="https://sonicboom.org.uk/privacy?utm_source=between&utm_medium=privacy&utm_campaign=sonicboom-privacy" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">privacy notice <span class="sr-only">(opens in new tab)</span></a>
            covers everything else we do, and the two are meant to be read together.
            Contact us at <a href="mailto:hello@sonicboom.org.uk?subject=Privacy%20-%20The%20Space%20Between">hello@sonicboom.org.uk</a> with any questions.
          </p>

          <h2>What we collect and why</h2>

          <h3>Email address (and optional name)</h3>
          <p>
            If you sign up to The Space Between community, we collect your email address
            and, optionally, your name. We use these to send you information about upcoming
            events and community news. Sign-ups are handled by Sonic Boom Music CIC's own mailing
            system, shared across our sites, and protected by <strong>Cloudflare Turnstile</strong>
            to keep spam and bots out. See Cloudflare's privacy policy at
            <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/privacypolicy/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">cloudflare.com/privacypolicy <span class="sr-only">(opens in new tab)</span></a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Lawful basis:</strong> consent. You can unsubscribe at any time using
            the link at the bottom of any email from us.
          </p>

          <h3>Postcode (optional)</h3>
          <p>
            We may ask for your postcode when you join the community or take part in the
            venue-demand poll. This is always optional and is never required. We use
            postcodes in aggregate form only, to understand how many responses come from
            each local area (e.g. how many from DE13, DE14). Individual postcodes are
            not stored against your personal record.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Lawful basis:</strong> consent. Your postcode is handled in the same
            way as your email: you can ask us to delete it at any time.
          </p>

          <h3>Venue-demand poll responses</h3>
          <p>
            If you answer "Would you use a permanent grassroots music venue in Burton?",
            we record your choice (Yes / Maybe) and the date. We use this to build the
            demand evidence for our venue campaign. Your individual response is stored
            against a technical identifier (not your name or email) and combined with
            other responses to create an aggregate total.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Lawful basis:</strong> legitimate interests (building evidence for a
            community venue campaign). You can ask us to delete your response at any time.
          </p>

          <h3>Event feedback and check-in</h3>
          <p>
            After each event we invite you to answer five quick questions at
            <a href="/feedback">/feedback</a>: how you heard about the event, how many of our
            events you've been to, the first half of your postcode (optional), whether you'd use
            a permanent grassroots music venue in Burton, and what you'd want from one (optional).
            At some free events you can also check in with a QR code, telling us how many of you
            there are and, optionally, whether it's your first Sonic Boom Music CIC event and the first
            half of your postcode.
          </p>
          <p>
            These answers are anonymous. We don't ask for your name or email, we only ever keep
            the first half of a postcode (e.g. DE14), and we don't store your IP address with
            your answers. Please don't include names or contact details in the free-text answer.
            We use the answers to plan future events, to build the evidence for our venue
            ambition, and to report to Arts Council England, who fund this programme. Reports
            only ever use combined figures and short anonymous quotes.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Lawful basis:</strong> legitimate interests (evaluating a publicly funded
            programme). Because the answers can't be linked to you, we can't find or delete an
            individual response, which is also why they're safe to keep for future planning.
          </p>

          <h3>Comments on our social media posts</h3>
          <p>
            People leave public comments on our Facebook and Instagram posts. We read them, and we keep a
            copy of the comments on our own posts so we can see what people say about the events and a
            possible venue, and count themes such as first-time visitors, families and what people would
            like to see. We keep the comment, the date, the post it was on and the event it relates to.
            We don't keep your username or profile name with it. We do keep the comment's reference number
            on the platform, so that we can reply and can tell if you've deleted it. That means the
            record is stripped of your name but isn't fully anonymous: someone with the reference could
            find your comment on the platform.
          </p>
          <p>
            We may use an AI assistant (Claude, from Anthropic) to suggest which themes a comment shows. It only suggests: a person checks and saves every tag before it is counted. In reports and on this website we use comments as combined counts and themes. We don't
            publish a comment word for word, or with a name, unless you have told us we can (for example
            by replying to us or by sharing your story at <a href="/feedback/story">/feedback/story</a>),
            because the words of a public comment can lead back to you. Otherwise we paraphrase. We never
            use comments from private messages, from other people's pages, or from anyone we think is under 16.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Lawful basis:</strong> legitimate interests (understanding the audience for a publicly
            funded programme and building the evidence for our venue ambition). You can object, ask us to
            delete a comment we hold, or ask us not to use it, by emailing hello@sonicboom.org.uk. If you
            delete the comment on Facebook or Instagram, we delete our copy the next time we check. The
            platforms (Meta) handle the original comment under their own privacy policies.
          </p>

          <h3>Your stories</h3>
          <p>
            If you share a story at <a href="/feedback/story">/feedback/story</a>, we collect the
            story, how you'd like to be credited (optional), a line about you (optional), which event
            it's about (optional) and your email (optional). We only use your email to check details
            with you before sharing, never publish it or add it to a mailing list, and delete it as
            soon as your story has been reviewed. We publish a story on this website only if you
            tick the box to say we can, and on social media only if you tick that box too.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Lawful basis:</strong> consent. You can withdraw it at any time by emailing
            hello@sonicboom.org.uk, and we'll take your story down and delete it.
          </p>

          <h3 id="media-consent">Photos, video, audio and your words</h3>
          <p>
            At our events we may take photos, film, record audio and collect people's words (for
            example a quote, or an interview) to tell the story of live music in Burton. If you're
            happy for us to use you in these, you can say so at
            <a href="/feedback/consent">/feedback/consent</a> (the QR code on our posters). We collect
            your name, the media you're happy with (photos, video, audio, your words), where we can
            use it (this website, Sonic Boom Music CIC's social media, printed posters, leaflets and
            press, and reports and applications to our funders), which event (optional) and your
            email (optional, only so we can check with you or act if you withdraw). If a parent or
            carer gives permission for a child under 16, we also keep the child's first name.
            Our organisation-wide notice explains that we photograph and film at our events to promote
            them, with signs where it's happening, and that you can ask us to remove an image of you.
            This form goes further: where we want to feature you clearly, or record your voice or quote
            you, or share something of a child, we ask for your explicit permission here. Anyone who
            doesn't want to be photographed, filmed or recorded can tell the person with the camera and
            we'll keep them out of it.
          </p>
          <p>
            We only use your photos, video, audio or words in the ways you ticked. When something is
            published on social media, the platform (for example Facebook or Instagram, run by Meta)
            handles it under its own privacy policy. Withdrawing means we stop using it and take it
            down from places we control; we can't recall printed material already distributed, and
            we can't control copies others have made from a public post.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Lawful basis:</strong> consent. You can withdraw it at any time by emailing
            <a href="mailto:hello@sonicboom.org.uk?subject=Withdrawing%20my%20permission%20-%20The%20Space%20Between">hello@sonicboom.org.uk</a>.
            Your form is visible only to the Sonic Boom Music CIC delivery team, so they know what
            they may share.
          </p>

          <h3>Analytics</h3>
          <p>
            This website uses <strong>Cloudflare Web Analytics</strong>, which does not use
            cookies, does not track individuals across sites, and does not store personal data.
            It measures aggregate page views and referrers only. There is no cookie banner on
            this site because none is required.
          </p>

          <h3>Which links work</h3>
          <p>
            The links we share in posts, emails and QR codes carry simple tags saying where they were shared (for
            example "instagram"). When a page is opened through one of those links, or someone signs up, votes, answers
            the survey, checks in, shares a story, gives photo and video permission or taps to join WhatsApp from that page, we add one to a daily total
            for that link. That's all we keep: totals per link per day. No cookies, nothing stored on your device, and
            nothing that identifies you or links your actions together.
          </p>

          <h3>Technical data</h3>
          <p>
            Like all websites, this site is served via Cloudflare's network. Cloudflare
            may process your IP address as part of routing web traffic. See Cloudflare's
            privacy policy at <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/privacypolicy/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">cloudflare.com/privacypolicy <span class="sr-only">(opens in new tab)</span></a>.
          </p>

          <h2>Tickets and event registration</h2>
          <p>
            Ticketed events use <strong>Ticket Tailor</strong> as our ticketing platform.
            When you buy a ticket or register for an event, you're sharing data with
            Ticket Tailor. See their privacy policy at <a href="https://www.tickettailor.com/privacy-policy" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">tickettailor.com/privacy-policy <span class="sr-only">(opens in new tab)</span></a>.
            We receive booking information (name, email, ticket type) to manage event
            attendance.
          </p>

          <h2>How long we keep your data</h2>
          <p>We only keep personal data for as long as we need it for the reason we collected it.</p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Email subscribers:</strong> until you unsubscribe, or 24 months after you last opened or clicked one of our emails, whichever comes first. You can also ask us to delete your data at any time.</li>
            <li><strong>Event booking records:</strong> up to two years after the event, for evaluation and reporting (including Arts Council England grant conditions). Ticketing accounting records are kept for six years, as set out in our organisation-wide notice.</li>
            <li><strong>Venue poll responses:</strong> for the duration of the venue campaign (currently expected to run until at least 2028), then reviewed.</li>
            <li><strong>Survey answers and check-ins:</strong> these can't be linked to you, so they can't be deleted individually. We keep the answers for planning and reporting, and the daily link counts as totals only.</li>
            <li><strong>Copies of social media comments:</strong> deleted 12 months after they were posted, or sooner if you delete the original or ask us to. The combined counts and themes we've drawn from them contain no personal data and are kept.</li>
            <li><strong>Stories:</strong> your email is deleted as soon as the story is reviewed. The story stays on the website until you ask us to take it down.</li>
            <li><strong>Photo, video, audio and words permission forms:</strong> for as long as we use the material they cover, so we can show we had permission and honour a withdrawal. If you withdraw, we stop using the material and keep a note that you withdrew, so it isn't used again. We review these forms at least once a year and delete any whose material we no longer use.</li>
          </ul>

          <h2>Who we share your data with</h2>
          <p>
            We use the following processors:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Sonic Boom Music CIC's shared mailer</strong>: email delivery, protected by Cloudflare Turnstile</li>
            <li><strong>Ticket Tailor</strong>: event ticketing and registration</li>
            <li><strong>Cloudflare</strong>: website hosting, analytics (cookieless), bot protection</li>
            <li><strong>Facebook and Instagram (Meta)</strong>: where you've said we can share your photos, video, audio or words on social media, and as the source of the public comments on our own posts that we read (see above)</li>
            <li><strong>Anthropic (Claude)</strong>: an AI assistant that may read the text of a comment, with no name, to suggest themes</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            We do not sell your data. We do not share it with third parties for marketing.
            We may share aggregate, anonymised statistics (e.g. "500 local residents have
            expressed interest in a music venue") with funders, the council, or press as
            part of the venue campaign.
          </p>

          <h2>Your rights</h2>
          <p>
            Under UK GDPR, you have the right to:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>Access the data we hold about you</li>
            <li>Correct inaccurate data</li>
            <li>Ask us to delete your data</li>
            <li>Restrict how we use your data</li>
            <li>Receive your data in a portable format</li>
            <li>Object to processing based on legitimate interests</li>
            <li>Withdraw consent at any time (without affecting previous processing)</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            To exercise any of these rights, email
            <a href="mailto:hello@sonicboom.org.uk?subject=Data%20rights%20request%20-%20The%20Space%20Between">hello@sonicboom.org.uk</a>.
            We aim to respond within one month.
          </p>

          <h2>Complaints</h2>
          <p>
            If you're not satisfied with how we've handled your data, you have the right
            to complain to the Information Commissioner's Office (ICO) at
            <a href="https://ico.org.uk/make-a-complaint/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">ico.org.uk <span class="sr-only">(opens in new tab)</span></a>
            or by calling 0303 123 1113.
          </p>

          <h2>Changes to this notice</h2>
          <p>
            We'll update this page if our practices change. The date below shows when
            it was last reviewed.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Last updated: November 2026</strong>
          </p>

        </div>

        <div class="page-back">
          <a href="/" class="btn btn-ghost-dark">
            <span aria-hidden="true">←</span> Back to the site
          </a>
        </div>

      </div>
    </div>

  </main>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  /* ── Page hero ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  .page-hero {
    background: var(--ink);
    padding-block: clamp(var(--space-10), 8vw, var(--space-16));
    border-bottom: 3px solid var(--line);
  }

  .page-hero__inner {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-5);
  }

  .page-hero__back {
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--body);
    text-decoration: none;
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.4em;
    transition: color 150ms var(--ease-out);
    width: fit-content;
  }
  .page-hero__back:hover { color: var(--paper); }
  .page-hero__back:focus-visible {
    outline: 3px solid var(--ember);
    outline-offset: 4px;
    border-radius: 2px;
  }

  .page-hero__title {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: clamp(2.25rem, 7vw, 4.5rem);
    font-weight: 800;
    letter-spacing: -0.03em;
    font-optical-sizing: auto;
    color: var(--paper);
    line-height: 1.0;
    margin: 0;
  }

  .page-hero__sub {
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: clamp(1rem, 2.5vw, 1.2rem);
    line-height: 1.65;
    color: var(--body);
    max-width: 56ch;
    margin: 0;
  }

  /* ── Content ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  .page-content {
    background: var(--paper);
    padding-block: clamp(var(--space-10), 8vw, var(--space-16));
  }

  .page-content__inner {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-12);
  }

  /* ── Prose ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  .prose {
    max-width: 68ch;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0;
  }

  .prose h2 {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: clamp(1.25rem, 3vw, 1.75rem);
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: -0.015em;
    font-optical-sizing: auto;
    color: var(--ink);
    line-height: 1.2;
    margin: var(--space-10) 0 var(--space-4);
  }
  .prose h2:first-child { margin-top: 0; }

  .prose h3 {
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: var(--text-base);
    font-weight: 700;
    color: var(--ink);
    margin: var(--space-6) 0 var(--space-2);
  }

  .prose p {
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: clamp(0.95rem, 2vw, 1.05rem);
    line-height: 1.75;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-4);
  }

  .prose ul {
    padding-left: var(--space-5);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-4);
  }
  .prose li {
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: clamp(0.95rem, 2vw, 1.05rem);
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-2);
  }

  .prose a {
    color: var(--red);
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-underline-offset: 2px;
    font-weight: 500;
  }
  .prose a:hover { color: var(--ink); }
  .prose a:focus-visible {
    outline: 3px solid var(--ember);
    outline-offset: 3px;
    border-radius: 2px;
  }

  .prose strong { color: var(--ink); font-weight: 700; }

  /* ── Back link ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  .page-back {
    padding-top: var(--space-6);
    border-top: 1px solid rgba(26, 18, 18, 0.1);
  }
</style>
